--TEST--
A `Core\Task\Channel`'s bound asserted on both sides: the last send that fits returns without the consumer running, and the first that does not fit only returns after it has — `rule:concurrency/all-answers-a-typed-shape`'s children over `nvs_stdlib::channel`'s bound
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A send is observed to have waited without asking the channel anything about
// itself: `Core\Task\Channel` deliberately has no `count` and no `isFull`,
// because either answer is stale before the caller reads it. What a producer
// can see is whether the *consumer* made progress in the middle of its own
// send, which is what backpressure means from the inside.
class Log {
    public static int $taken = 0;
    public static string $waits = "";
}

// Capacity two against three sends: two fit, the third cannot.
var $chan = new Core\Task\Channel<int>(2);

// Both halves are children of this call, so `rule:concurrency/nothing-is-still-running-when-a-call-returns`'s promise applies —
// `all` does not return with either still running, which is what lets the
// counters below be read as final.
Core\Task::all({
    produce: fn(): int => {
        foreach (Core\Arr::range(1, 3) as int $i) {
            var $before = Log::$taken;
            $chan->send($i);
            if (Log::$taken > $before) {
                Log::$waits = Log::$waits . "w";
            } else {
                Log::$waits = Log::$waits . "-";
            }
        }
        // Without this the consumer's `foreach` never ends: an empty channel
        // that is still open is a wait, not an end.
        $chan->close();
        return 3;
    },
    consume: fn(): int => {
        foreach ($chan as int $v) {
            Log::$taken += 1;
        }
        return Log::$taken;
    },
});

// The bound, from both sides: `-` for a send that fit, `w` for one that had to
// wait for room. A channel that grew instead of waiting prints `---`; one that
// waited a send early prints `-w-`.
echo Log::$waits, "\n";
// Nothing is dropped by the wait, and nothing is left behind by the close.
echo Log::$taken, "\n";
--EXPECT--
--w
3
